ful here because it lists the commands that can be rolled back. Without knowing which commands were executed, I can't effectively use rollback_command. \n\nSo, the first step should be to retrieve the list of previous commands using get_previous_rollbackable_cmd. Once I have that list, I can determine the order and which commands to undo. However, the user's initial instruction says to run the rollback tool as many times as possible until there are no more operations to undo. But if I don't know the commands, maybe I should start by getting the list. \n\nWait, but the rollback_command can be called multiple times to undo previous commands in reverse order. However, if there are no commands to rollback, it might not do anything. So maybe the correct approach is to first check the available commands. But the user's task says to rollback until no more operations are left. So perhaps the initial step is to use get_previous_rollbackable_cmd to find out which commands are available. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the rollback_command can be called directly, but if there are no commands, it would just exit. But the user wants to ensure that all changes are rolled back. So the safest approach is to first get the list of commands that can be rolled back. That way, I can process them in the correct order. \n\nWait, but the user's instruction says to run the rollback tool as many times as possible. So maybe the first step is to call rollback_command repeatedly until it can't anymore. But if there are no commands, it would stop. However, without knowing the commands, maybe the system has some history. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the get_previous_rollbackable_cmd is necessary to list the commands, then use rollback_command on each. But the user's task says to run the rollback tool (which is rollback_command) as many times as possible. So perhaps the correct first step is to call get_previous_rollbackable_cmd to find out which commands are available, then proceed to rollback each. \n\nBut