he last executed command, so if there are multiple commands to rollback, they need to be addressed in reverse order. \n\nBut wait, the user might have multiple commands. The get_previous_rollbackable_cmd might have given a list, so maybe they need to loop through each command and rollback each one. However, the rollback_command tool, when called multiple times, automatically rolls back previous commands in reverse order. So the user can just call rollback_command once for each command they want to undo. \n\nAlternatively, if there's a list of commands, maybe they need to process each one. But the tool's description says that calling it multiple times will reverse the order of prior execution. So if the previous commands were cmd1, cmd2, cmd3, then calling rollback_command three times would undo cmd3, then cmd2, then cmd1. \n\nSo the user should now use the rollback_command tool. The arguments are empty, as the tool doesn't require any parameters. The goal is to undo the last operation, so starting with the most recent command. \n\nWait, but the user might need to confirm if there are any commands to rollback. Since the previous step retrieved the list, assuming there are commands, the next step is to rollback them. So the correct tool here is rollback_command. \n\nTherefore, the tool choice is rollback_command, and the tool call would be to execute it once. If there are multiple commands, subsequent calls would handle the rest. But the user needs to keep calling it until there are no more operations to undo. \n\nSo the answer is to call rollback_command.\n</think>\n\nrollback_command\n\nThe rollback_command tool is necessary to undo the previously executed kubectl commands that caused the system to deviate from its initial state. Since the get_previous_rollbackable_cmd tool has already identified the list of eligible commands, the next logical step is to reverse them in reverse chronological order. This ensures the system is restored to its original state without leaving residual changes. Each call to rollback_command will undo the last operation, so we start with the most recent command.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=538, prompt_tokens=777, total_tokens=1315, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd13-e378-7120-9a85-351dbdfd7177-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 777, 'output_tokens': 538, 'total_tokens': 1315}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='872654ce-10f8-4997-8944-bba04cd5cfa0')]]
